      {
        "ballot_id": "B004",
        "laureates": [
          "Alessandra Buonanno",
          "Frans Pretorius",
          "Thibault Damour"
        ],
        "citation": "For effective-one-body theory and numerical relativity simulations that made reliable predictions of binary black-hole mergers possible."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B007",
    "B051"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I retain B004, my round-one choice. The effective-one-body framework and stable numerical simulations address the same difficult task from complementary directions: predicting binary black-hole mergers. Their predictions have been tested against observed gravitational-wave signals, supporting the achievement's maturity. A citation confined to these foundational methods gives the work a coherent scope and credits identifiable contributions by Damour, Buonanno, and Pretorius.",
    "case_against": "The work developed within a broad community, and the boundary between foundational advances and later waveform development remains consequential for individual credit. The proposed three names correspond to complementary contributions, but the historical record must support that division before attribution is settled. I see no reason from this exchange to change my configuration; the attribution question remains open.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson supports B004's complementary theoretical and numerical advances, while cautioning that the claim must distinguish foundational methods from later waveform development and the wider community's work.",
        "response": "I agree that this is the central attribution question. The citation should cover the effective-one-body framework and the breakthrough stable simulations, not later waveform development or gravitational-wave detection. That focused division makes the proposed trio defensible, subject to historical review of each contribution."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson prefers the pulsar discovery, emphasizing its maturity and lasting significance while calling for historical review of Bell Burnell's role within the observing team; she also lists B004 as an alternative.",
        "response": "Her distinction between a mature discovery and unsettled individual credit is useful here too. B004's observational tests support its maturity, but do not by themselves resolve who merits credit for its foundational methods. I favor B004 because its analytic and numerical contributions converge on one bounded predictive task; the proposed attribution still needs the historical scrutiny she calls for in B009."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The historical boundary between the foundational effective-one-body and numerical-relativity advances and later waveform development needs precise review, including evidence supporting the proposed division of credit among Damour, Buonanno, and Pretorius."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "My profile notes co-authored work with Ursula Keller's group, whose contributions are relevant to shortlisted B001. B001 is not in my preferred configuration. No comparable personal or professional connection to the B004 candidates is apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
